    <main role="main">
      
<div class="jumbotron">
  <div class="container">
    <h1 class="brand-white brand-mono-bold">Reverse Engineering</h1>
    <br>
    <h2 class="module-dojo"><a class="brand-white brand-mono-bold" href="/dojo/intro-re-pwn~5933fc84">Intro to Reverse Engineering &amp; Binary Exploitation<span class="brand-dot">.</span></a></h2>

    
  </div>
</div>
<div class="container">

  
  <div>
    
    <p>Combine static analysis and debugging to understand small programs without
seeing their source code.</p>
<p>New to this? Read the <strong>Start here</strong> lessons below before the first
challenge. They walk through a complete example, from a program you know
nothing about to the input it wants.</p>

  </div>
  <br>
  

  
  

  <div class="accordion" id="module-content">
    
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-1">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-1" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-1">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">Start here: what reverse engineering is</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-1" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-1" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <h2>What this module is about</h2>
<p><strong>Reverse engineering</strong> (RE) means working out what a program does when you
have only the compiled program, not the source code someone wrote it from.
Every challenge in this module is a small Linux program that checks
something you type: a number, a password, or a table position. You will read
its machine code, run it under a debugger, and turn what you learn into the
input it accepts. Every later module builds on this: before you can exploit a
bug, you have to find it and understand the code around it.</p>
<p>This module assumes you have done <strong>Assembly Basics</strong> (registers, <code>mov</code>,
<code>cmp</code>, jumps) and <strong>Looking Inside Programs</strong> (<code>strings</code>, <code>objdump</code>,
<code>ltrace</code>). If a line like <code>mov eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]</code> means nothing to you
yet, go back to those first.</p>
<h2>The workflow</h2>
<p>Reverse engineering is a loop of guessing and checking. Most problems go
through four steps, often more than once:</p>
<pre><code> 1. LOOK            2. STATIC ANALYSIS     3. DYNAMIC ANALYSIS    4. CONFIRM
 run it once,       read the code          run it under a         compute the
 file, strings,     without running it:    debugger, stop it,     answer (often
 ltrace             objdump, Ghidra        inspect and change     in Python) and
                                           values: gdb            give it to the
                                                                  real program
   |                     |                       |                     |
   +----&gt; form a guess --+----&gt; test the guess --+----&gt; works? done.   |
               ^                                        no? go back ---+
</code></pre>
<ol>
<li><strong>Look.</strong> Spend two minutes on cheap questions. What kind of file is it
(<code>file</code>)? What text is inside (<code>strings</code>)? What does it print when you run
it? Which library functions does it call (<code>ltrace</code>)? You are collecting
clues, not answers.</li>
<li><strong>Static analysis</strong> means reading the program without running it. A
<strong>disassembler</strong> (<code>objdump</code>) shows the machine instructions. A
<strong>decompiler</strong> (Ghidra) turns those instructions back into approximate C,
which is faster to read but can be wrong in small ways.</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic analysis</strong> means running the program and watching it. A
<strong>debugger</strong> (<code>gdb</code>) can stop the program at any instruction, show you
registers and memory at that moment, and change them.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm.</strong> Feed your answer to the real program. The program is the
judge, not your notes. If it says no, one of your guesses was wrong; go
back and find which one.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Tools in this dojo</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tool</th>
<th>What it is for</th>
<th>How to start it</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>file</code>, <code>strings</code>, <code>readelf</code></td>
<td>first look at a program</td>
<td><code>readelf -h /challenge/NAME</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>objdump</code></td>
<td>disassemble</td>
<td><code>objdump -d -M intel /challenge/NAME | more</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>ltrace</code></td>
<td>list library calls as they happen</td>
<td><code>ltrace /challenge/NAME</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ghidra</td>
<td>decompile to C (graphical)</td>
<td><code>ghidraRun</code> from the Desktop workspace</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ghidra headless</td>
<td>decompile from a terminal</td>
<td><code>analyzeHeadless</code> (lesson &quot;Ghidra and gdb in this dojo&quot;)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>radare2 + Ghidra's decompiler</td>
<td>quick decompile in a terminal</td>
<td><code>r2 -A -qc 'pdg @ main' /challenge/NAME</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>gdb</code> with pwndbg</td>
<td>debugger</td>
<td><code>gdb /challenge/NAME</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>python3</code></td>
<td>calculator and small scripts</td>
<td><code>python3</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Ghidra is installed in <code>/opt/ghidra</code>. Its own beginner course and cheat
sheet are in <code>/opt/ghidra/docs/GhidraClass/Beginner/</code> and
<code>/opt/ghidra/docs/CheatSheet.html</code>.</p>
<h2>A suggested order</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>xor-key</strong>: function names are present; one <code>xor</code>, one <code>cmp</code>.</li>
<li><strong>stripped</strong>: the names are gone; find <code>main</code> yourself.</li>
<li><strong>simple-crackme</strong>: a decode loop with a one-byte key.</li>
<li><strong>encoded-string</strong>: a decode loop where each byte gets a different key.</li>
<li><strong>multi-func</strong>: a table of function addresses.</li>
<li><strong>gdb-runtime</strong>: the answer exists only while the program runs.</li>
</ol>
<p>Read the next lessons in order. &quot;Worked example&quot; puts everything together
on a toy program that is not one of the challenges.</p>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-2">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-2" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-2">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">Concepts: finding main in a stripped program</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-2" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-2" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <h2>Symbols, and what stripping removes</h2>
<p>When <code>gcc</code> builds a program it keeps a <strong>symbol table</strong>: a list of names
(<code>main</code>, <code>check</code>, global variable names) and the address of each. Tools use
it to label the disassembly with <code>&lt;main&gt;:</code> and to let you type
<code>break main</code> in gdb. The program does not need these names to run.</p>
<p><code>strip</code> deletes the symbol table. The result is a <strong>stripped</strong> binary. Every
instruction is still there; only the labels are gone. <code>file</code> tells you:</p>
<pre><code>toylock-stripped: ELF 64-bit LSB executable, x86-64, ... stripped
</code></pre>
<p>Names of <strong>library functions</strong> such as <code>printf</code> and <code>memcmp</code> survive,
because the program needs them to find those functions in the C library
when it starts.</p>
<h2>How a C program starts</h2>
<p><code>main</code> is not the first code that runs. The <strong>entry point</strong> is the address
where Linux starts executing the program. It is written in the file's
header, and it points at a small piece of startup code called <code>_start</code>:</p>
<pre><code> Linux loads the program
         |
         v
 _start                    at the entry point address (readelf -h)
         |  puts the ADDRESS OF main into rdi, the first-argument register
         v
 __libc_start_main(main, ...)      inside the C library
         |  sets up the library, then calls the function it was given
         v
 main(argc, argv, envp)    the code the programmer wrote
         |
         v
 exit(whatever main returned)
</code></pre>
<p>On x86-64 Linux the first six arguments of a function call travel in the
registers <code>rdi</code>, <code>rsi</code>, <code>rdx</code>, <code>rcx</code>, <code>r8</code>, <code>r9</code>, in that order. So the
address of <code>main</code> is whatever <code>_start</code> puts into <code>rdi</code> right before it calls
<code>__libc_start_main</code>.</p>
<h2>The recipe</h2>
<ol>
<li><code>readelf -h PROGRAM</code> and read the line <code>Entry point address</code>.</li>
<li><code>objdump -d -M intel PROGRAM | more</code>, then search for that address
(type <code>/</code> and the address without <code>0x</code>, then Enter). <code>more</code> shows one
screen at a time: Space for the next screen, <code>/</code> to search, <code>q</code> to quit.</li>
<li>A few lines later you will see <code>mov rdi,ADDRESS</code>, then a <code>call</code>, then
<code>hlt</code>. That <code>ADDRESS</code> is <code>main</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>The worked example shows this on a real program. Two warnings:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>objdump invents labels in stripped programs.</strong> It names every address
after the nearest symbol it still knows, so you will see nonsense such as
<code>call 40124b &lt;fgets@plt+0x16b&gt;</code>. That call does not go anywhere near
<code>fgets</code>. Ignore the text in <code>&lt; &gt;</code> and use the number.</li>
<li>All programs in this module are built as <strong>non-PIE</strong>, so they load at the
same fixed addresses every run and the address in <code>mov rdi,ADDRESS</code> is the
real one. A <strong>PIE</strong> (position-independent executable) is loaded at a
random address each run; its <code>_start</code> uses <code>lea rdi,[rip+0x...]</code> instead,
and the comment objdump prints after it holds the offset of <code>main</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ghidra and radare2 apply this same recipe for you. In a stripped program
Ghidra shows the startup code as <code>entry</code>, and the decompiled <code>entry</code> reads
<code>__libc_start_main(FUN_004012ac, ...)</code>: the first argument is <code>main</code>.
Knowing the recipe still matters, because it tells you what to check when a
tool guesses wrong.</p>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-3">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-3" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-3" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-3">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">Concepts: encode loops, XOR, and lookup tables</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-3" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-3" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <h2>Why programs hide strings</h2>
<p>If a password sits in the program as plain text, <code>strings</code> shows it in one
second. So simple programs <strong>encode</strong> it: they store a scrambled version and
use a small, reversible rule to turn your input into the scrambled form, or
the scrambled form back into text (<strong>decode</strong>). This is not real encryption.
Anyone who reads the rule can undo it, and reading the rule is your job.</p>
<h2>XOR</h2>
<p><strong>XOR</strong> (exclusive OR, <code>^</code> in C and Python, <code>xor</code> in assembly) compares two
numbers bit by bit. Each result bit is 1 when the two input bits differ:</p>
<pre><code>  bit A  bit B  A^B           0x61  = 0110 0001   the letter 'a'
    0      0     0          ^ 0x13  = 0001 0011   a key byte
    0      1     1          = 0x72  = 0111 0010   scrambled
    1      0     1          ^ 0x13  = 0001 0011   the same key again
    1      1     0          = 0x61  = 0110 0001   back to 'a'
</code></pre>
<p>The right-hand side shows the property that makes XOR popular: applying the
same key twice gives back what you started with. If <code>scrambled = plain ^ key</code>,
then <code>plain = scrambled ^ key</code>. You undo XOR with XOR.</p>
<h2>What a loop looks like at <code>-O0</code></h2>
<p>The programs here are compiled without optimization (<code>-O0</code>), so every
<code>for</code> loop has the same shape. The loop counter lives on the stack:</p>
<pre><code>       mov  DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4],0x0    ; i = 0
       jmp  CHECK                      ; test the condition first
BODY:  ...                             ; one round of work, uses i
       add  DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4],0x1    ; i = i + 1
CHECK: mov  eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]
       cmp  eax,LIMIT
       jl   BODY                       ; go back while i &lt; LIMIT
</code></pre>
<p>To find a loop, look for a jump that goes <strong>backwards</strong> (to a smaller
address). Then count the rounds carefully: <code>jl</code> (jump if less) means
<code>i &lt; LIMIT</code>, which is <code>LIMIT</code> rounds. <code>jle</code> (jump if less or equal) means
<code>i &lt;= LIMIT</code>, which is <code>LIMIT + 1</code> rounds.</p>
<p>An <strong>encode loop</strong> body has three parts: read one byte (<code>movzx eax,BYTE PTR [...]</code>), mix it with something (<code>xor</code>, <code>add</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>imul</code>), and write one
byte (<code>mov BYTE PTR [...],dl</code>). Find those three and you have the rule.</p>
<h2>Lookup tables</h2>
<p>A <strong>lookup table</strong> is an array of constants stored in the program, read at a
position (an <strong>index</strong>) the code computes. The bytes sit next to each other
in memory:</p>
<pre><code> address   0x402008  0x402009  0x40200a  0x40200b
 value       0x13      0x37      0x42      0x7f
 index       [0]       [1]       [2]       [3]
</code></pre>
<p>In assembly, reading <code>table[index]</code> looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>movzx  eax,BYTE PTR [rax+0x402008]    ; one byte from (0x402008 + rax)
</code></pre>
<p>The constant is the table's address and the register is the index. For
bigger entries the index is multiplied by the entry size: <code>[rax*4+ADDR]</code> for
4-byte numbers, <code>[rax*8+ADDR]</code> for 8-byte values. An 8-byte value is often an
<strong>address of a function</strong>. A table of function addresses followed by a
<code>call rax</code> (an <strong>indirect call</strong>, where the target comes from a register) is
how a program picks which function to run based on a number.</p>
<p><strong><code>i % 4</code> in disassembly.</strong> A key that repeats every 4 bytes uses
<code>table[i % 4]</code>. gcc writes <code>%</code> as a strange-looking sequence, because it has
to handle negative numbers too:</p>
<pre><code>sar    eax,0x1f
shr    eax,0x1e
add    edx,eax
and    edx,0x3        ; &lt;- the 3 means &quot;% 4&quot; (for i &gt;= 0, i % 4 == i &amp; 3)
sub    edx,eax
</code></pre>
<p>When you see <code>and REG,0x3</code> (or <code>0x7</code>, <code>0xf</code>) next to shifts, think &quot;remainder
after dividing by 4 (or 8, or 16)&quot;.</p>
<h2>Undoing an encode rule</h2>
<p>Undo the steps <strong>in reverse order</strong>, each with its opposite:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>the program did</th>
<th>you do</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>x ^ k</code></td>
<td><code>x ^ k</code> (XOR undoes itself)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>x + k</code></td>
<td><code>x - k</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>x - k</code></td>
<td><code>x + k</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>the last step</td>
<td>first</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Machine registers wrap around: a byte that goes past <code>0xff</code> starts again at
<code>0x00</code>. Python numbers never wrap, so after each <code>+</code> or <code>-</code> add <code>&amp; 0xff</code> to
keep only the lowest 8 bits, the same as the program does.</p>
<p>Notice which direction the program works in. If it <strong>decodes</strong> stored bytes
and compares the result with your input, run the decode yourself (or read
the decoded text in gdb). If it <strong>encodes your input</strong> and compares that with
stored bytes, you must invert the rule. The worked example is the second kind.</p>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-4">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-4" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-4" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-4">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">Ghidra and gdb in this dojo</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-4" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-4" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <h2>Ghidra, the graphical way</h2>
<p>Open the <strong>Desktop</strong> workspace in pwn.college, open a terminal there, and run
<code>ghidraRun</code>. Then:</p>
<ol>
<li>Accept the license, close the tips window.</li>
<li><strong>File → New Project → Non-Shared Project</strong>, pick a folder in your home
directory and a name, <strong>Finish</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>File → Import File</strong>, choose <code>/challenge/NAME</code>, <strong>OK</strong>, <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
<li>Double-click the imported program. Answer <strong>Yes</strong> to &quot;Analyze?&quot;, then
click <strong>Analyze</strong>.</li>
<li>On the left, <strong>Symbol Tree → Functions</strong> lists every function. Click one.
The middle panel (<strong>Listing</strong>) shows its assembly; the right panel
(<strong>Decompile</strong>) shows Ghidra's C. Clicking a line in one panel highlights
the matching lines in the other.</li>
</ol>
<p>Two keys you will use: <strong>L</strong> renames whatever is under the cursor (rename
<code>FUN_0040124b</code> to <code>check</code> once you know what it does), and <strong>G</strong> jumps to an
address you type.</p>
<h2>Ghidra without the graphics</h2>
<p><code>analyzeHeadless</code> runs the same analysis from a terminal. It needs a small
script to print the decompiled C. Save this as
<code>~/ghidra_scripts/Decompile.java</code> (for example with <code>nano</code>):</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">// Write Ghidra's decompiled C to ~/&lt;program&gt;.decomp.c
// Name functions after the script to pick them; name none to get every function.
import ghidra.app.decompiler.DecompInterface;
import ghidra.app.script.GhidraScript;
import ghidra.program.model.listing.Function;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Decompile extends GhidraScript {
    @Override
    public void run() throws Exception {
        List&lt;String&gt; wanted = Arrays.asList(getScriptArgs());
        File outFile = new File(System.getProperty(&quot;user.home&quot;),
                                currentProgram.getName() + &quot;.decomp.c&quot;);
        DecompInterface decompiler = new DecompInterface();
        decompiler.openProgram(currentProgram);
        try (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(outFile)) {
            for (Function f : currentProgram.getFunctionManager().getFunctions(true)) {
                if (f.isThunk() || f.isExternal()) continue;
                if (!wanted.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; !wanted.contains(f.getName())) continue;
                out.println(decompiler.decompileFunction(f, 60, monitor)
                                      .getDecompiledFunction().getC());
            }
        }
        println(&quot;wrote &quot; + outFile);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then run it (about 10 seconds for a small program):</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">mkdir -p ~/ghidra-projects
analyzeHeadless ~/ghidra-projects scratch -import /challenge/NAME -deleteProject \
    -scriptPath ~/ghidra_scripts -postScript Decompile.java &gt; ~/ghidra.log 2&gt;&amp;1
more ~/NAME.decomp.c
</code></pre>
<p><code>-deleteProject</code> throws the project away afterwards, so you can run the same
command again. If <code>NAME.decomp.c</code> is missing, read <code>~/ghidra.log</code>.</p>
<h2>The fastest way: radare2 with Ghidra's decompiler</h2>
<p>radare2 (<code>r2</code>) is another reverse-engineering tool. The copy here includes
Ghidra's decompiler, behind the command <code>pdg</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">r2 -A -qc 'pdg @ main' /challenge/NAME 2&gt;/dev/null
r2 -A -qc 'afl' /challenge/NAME 2&gt;/dev/null       # list the functions it found
</code></pre>
<p><code>-A</code> analyzes the program, <code>-q</code> quits afterwards, <code>-c</code> runs the command in
quotes, and <code>2&gt;/dev/null</code> hides the progress messages. radare2 finds <code>main</code>
even in a stripped program; other unnamed functions appear as
<code>fcn.ADDRESS</code>. Its names for variables differ from Ghidra's own (<code>arg1</code>
instead of <code>param_1</code>), but the logic is the same.</p>
<h2>gdb, the commands you need</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Command</th>
<th>What it does</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>gdb /challenge/NAME</code></td>
<td>load the program (it does not start yet)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>break *0x401228</code></td>
<td>stop when execution reaches that address. The <code>*</code> means &quot;address&quot;; without it gdb looks for a function name, and a stripped program has none.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>run</code></td>
<td>start the program. <code>run &lt; /tmp/in.txt</code> feeds a file as its input.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>continue</code> (<code>c</code>)</td>
<td>keep running until the next breakpoint</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>ni</code> / <code>si</code></td>
<td>run one instruction; <code>si</code> follows into calls, <code>ni</code> steps over them</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>x/1i $pc</code></td>
<td>show the instruction about to run (<code>$pc</code> is the current address)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>info registers</code></td>
<td>every register</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>p/x $eax</code></td>
<td>print one register in hex (<code>p $eax</code> for decimal)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>x/8bx ADDRESS</code></td>
<td>show 8 bytes of memory in hex (more in &quot;How to read the output&quot;)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>set $eax = 0</code></td>
<td>change a register</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>attach PID</code> / <code>detach</code></td>
<td>take control of an already-running process / let it go</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>kill</code>, <code>quit</code></td>
<td>stop the program / leave gdb</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>gdb here loads <strong>pwndbg</strong>, an add-on that redraws registers, the stack and
the next instructions every time the program stops. It makes the screen
busy but the commands above work the same.</p>
<p><strong>gdb and the flag.</strong> The challenge programs are <strong>setuid</strong>: they run with
extra permission so they can read <code>/flag</code>. Linux removes that permission when
a debugger starts the program. Use gdb to learn how the program works, then
run it normally with your answer to get the flag. (<strong>gdb-runtime</strong> is built
differently; its README explains how.)</p>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-5">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-5" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-5">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">Worked example: reversing a toy password check</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-5" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-5" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <p>This is a toy program written for this lesson. It is <strong>not</strong> one of the
challenges, and its rule is different from all of them. We pretend we have
never seen its source; the source is at the end. Every output below was
captured from real runs. Where we cut lines, we say so.</p>
<p>The program is <code>toylock-stripped</code>. It asks for a password.</p>
<h2>Step 1: look</h2>
<pre><code>$ file toylock-stripped
toylock-stripped: ELF 64-bit LSB executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, BuildID[sha1]=074a0d112cebca200cfecfc122f14d0e531efdc5, for GNU/Linux 3.2.0, stripped
</code></pre>
<p>64-bit Linux program (<strong>ELF</strong> is the Linux program file format),
<strong>executable</strong> (not PIE, so fixed addresses), <strong>stripped</strong> (no function
names).</p>
<pre><code>$ strings toylock-stripped        (excerpt: the lines that look like messages)
Password:
Door open.
Wrong.
</code></pre>
<p>The messages are there; the password is not. It is probably encoded.</p>
<pre><code>$ echo abcdefgh | ltrace ./toylock-stripped
printf(&quot;Password: &quot;)                             = 10
fgets(&quot;abcdefgh\n&quot;, 64, 0x70b90aa608e0)          = 0x7ffcf25abfc0
strcspn(&quot;abcdefgh\n&quot;, &quot;\n&quot;)                      = 8
strlen(&quot;abcdefgh&quot;)                               = 8
memcmp(0x7ffcf25abfa8, 0x402010, 8, 16)          = 15
puts(&quot;Wrong.&quot;)                                   = 7
Password: Wrong.
+++ exited (status 0) +++
</code></pre>
<p>Each line is one library call: the function, its arguments, and after <code>=</code>
what it returned. Clues:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>fgets</code> reads our line; <code>strcspn</code> finds the newline so it can be removed.</li>
<li><code>strlen</code> measures our input. Maybe the length is checked.</li>
<li><code>memcmp</code> compares 8 bytes at two addresses and returned 15 (not 0, so
they differ). One address (<code>0x7ff...</code>) is on the stack; the other,
<code>0x402010</code>, is a fixed address inside the program. Our text
<code>abcdefgh</code> is not passed to <code>memcmp</code> directly, so something changed it
first. (<code>memcmp</code> takes three arguments; ltrace guessed a fourth, <code>16</code>,
which is leftover junk.)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Guess:</strong> the program transforms our 8 characters and compares the result
with 8 stored bytes at <code>0x402010</code>.</p>
<h2>Step 2: find main</h2>
<pre><code>$ readelf -h toylock-stripped        (excerpt)
  Type:                              EXEC (Executable file)
  Entry point address:               0x4010f0
</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ objdump -d -M intel toylock-stripped        (excerpt, from 0x4010f0)
  4010f0:       f3 0f 1e fa             endbr64
  4010f4:       31 ed                   xor    ebp,ebp
  4010f6:       49 89 d1                mov    r9,rdx
  4010f9:       5e                      pop    rsi
  4010fa:       48 89 e2                mov    rdx,rsp
  4010fd:       48 83 e4 f0             and    rsp,0xfffffffffffffff0
  401101:       50                      push   rax
  401102:       54                      push   rsp
  401103:       45 31 c0                xor    r8d,r8d
  401106:       31 c9                   xor    ecx,ecx
  401108:       48 c7 c7 ac 12 40 00    mov    rdi,0x4012ac
  40110f:       ff 15 c3 2e 00 00       call   QWORD PTR [rip+0x2ec3]        # 403fd8 &lt;fgets@plt+0x2ef8&gt;
  401115:       f4                      hlt
</code></pre>
<p><code>mov rdi,0x4012ac</code>, then <code>call</code>, then <code>hlt</code>: <strong><code>main</code> is at <code>0x4012ac</code></strong>.
The label <code>&lt;fgets@plt+0x2ef8&gt;</code> is one of objdump's invented names. The call
really goes through the slot at <code>0x403fd8</code>, and <code>readelf -r</code> confirms that
slot belongs to <code>__libc_start_main</code>:</p>
<pre><code>$ readelf -r toylock-stripped | grep 403fd8
000000403fd8  000100000006 R_X86_64_GLOB_DAT 0000000000000000 __libc_start_main@GLIBC_2.34 + 0
</code></pre>
<h2>Step 3: static analysis with Ghidra</h2>
<p>This is Ghidra's decompiler output (captured with the headless script from
&quot;Ghidra and gdb in this dojo&quot;; the GUI's Decompile panel shows the same). Ghidra
calls <code>main</code> <code>FUN_004012ac</code>, after its address:</p>
<pre><code class="language-c">undefined8 FUN_004012ac(void)

{
  int iVar1;
  char *pcVar2;
  undefined8 uVar3;
  size_t sVar4;
  char local_48 [64];

  printf(&quot;Password: &quot;);
  pcVar2 = fgets(local_48,0x40,stdin);
  if (pcVar2 == (char *)0x0) {
    uVar3 = 1;
  }
  else {
    sVar4 = strcspn(local_48,&quot;\n&quot;);
    local_48[sVar4] = '\0';
    iVar1 = FUN_0040124b(local_48);
    if (iVar1 == 0) {
      puts(&quot;Wrong.&quot;);
    }
    else {
      puts(&quot;Door open.&quot;);
    }
    uVar3 = 0;
  }
  return uVar3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Read it top to bottom: read up to 64 bytes into <code>local_48</code>, cut off the
newline, call <code>FUN_0040124b</code> with our text. If it returns 0, &quot;Wrong.&quot;;
otherwise &quot;Door open.&quot;. So <code>FUN_0040124b</code> is the checker. In the GUI you
would now press <strong>L</strong> on it and rename it <code>check</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-c">bool FUN_0040124b(char *param_1)

{
  int iVar1;
  size_t sVar2;
  bool bVar3;
  undefined1 local_10 [8];

  sVar2 = strlen(param_1);
  if (sVar2 == 8) {
    FUN_004011d6(param_1,local_10,8);
    iVar1 = memcmp(local_10,&amp;DAT_00402010,8);
    bVar3 = iVar1 == 0;
  }
  else {
    bVar3 = false;
  }
  return bVar3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This matches the ltrace guess. The password must be exactly 8 characters.
<code>FUN_004011d6</code> is given our text, an 8-byte buffer <code>local_10</code>, and the number
8; then the buffer is compared with the 8 bytes at <code>0x402010</code>
(<code>DAT_00402010</code> is Ghidra's name for &quot;some data at this address&quot;). So
<code>FUN_004011d6</code> fills the buffer from our input: it is the encode step.</p>
<pre><code class="language-c">void FUN_004011d6(long param_1,long param_2,int param_3)

{
  int local_c;

  for (local_c = 0; local_c &lt; param_3; local_c = local_c + 1) {
    *(char *)(param_2 + local_c) =
         (char)local_c + *(char *)(param_1 + local_c) ^ (&amp;DAT_00402008)[local_c % 4];
  }
  return;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Rename in your head: <code>param_1</code> is <code>in</code>, <code>param_2</code> is <code>out</code>, <code>param_3</code> is <code>n</code>,
<code>local_c</code> is <code>i</code>, <code>DAT_00402008</code> is <code>KEY</code>. Then the line reads:</p>
<pre><code class="language-c">out[i] = (i + in[i]) ^ KEY[i % 4];
</code></pre>
<p>C does <code>+</code> before <code>^</code>, so the addition happens first. <code>KEY[i % 4]</code> is a
<strong>4-entry lookup table</strong> at <code>0x402008</code>: position 0 uses <code>KEY[0]</code>, position 4
uses <code>KEY[0]</code> again.</p>
<p><strong>Check the decompiler against the assembly</strong> when it matters. Here is the
same loop from objdump (excerpt; objdump's invented <code>&lt;fgets@plt+...&gt;</code> labels
left as they are):</p>
<pre><code>  4011e9:       c7 45 fc 00 00 00 00    mov    DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4],0x0
  4011f0:       eb 4d                   jmp    40123f &lt;fgets@plt+0x15f&gt;
  4011f2:       8b 45 fc                mov    eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]
  4011f5:       48 63 d0                movsxd rdx,eax
  4011f8:       48 8b 45 e8             mov    rax,QWORD PTR [rbp-0x18]
  4011fc:       48 01 d0                add    rax,rdx
  4011ff:       0f b6 00                movzx  eax,BYTE PTR [rax]
  401202:       89 c2                   mov    edx,eax
  401204:       8b 45 fc                mov    eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]
  401207:       01 d0                   add    eax,edx
  401209:       89 c1                   mov    ecx,eax
  40120b:       8b 55 fc                mov    edx,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]
  40120e:       89 d0                   mov    eax,edx
  401210:       c1 f8 1f                sar    eax,0x1f
  401213:       c1 e8 1e                shr    eax,0x1e
  401216:       01 c2                   add    edx,eax
  401218:       83 e2 03                and    edx,0x3
  40121b:       29 c2                   sub    edx,eax
  40121d:       89 d0                   mov    eax,edx
  40121f:       48 98                   cdqe
  401221:       0f b6 80 08 20 40 00    movzx  eax,BYTE PTR [rax+0x402008]
  401228:       31 c1                   xor    ecx,eax
  40122a:       8b 45 fc                mov    eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]
  40122d:       48 63 d0                movsxd rdx,eax
  401230:       48 8b 45 e0             mov    rax,QWORD PTR [rbp-0x20]
  401234:       48 01 d0                add    rax,rdx
  401237:       89 ca                   mov    edx,ecx
  401239:       88 10                   mov    BYTE PTR [rax],dl
  40123b:       83 45 fc 01             add    DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4],0x1
  40123f:       8b 45 fc                mov    eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]
  401242:       3b 45 dc                cmp    eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x24]
  401245:       7c ab                   jl     4011f2 &lt;fgets@plt+0x112&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Line by line, in groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>4011e9</code>–<code>4011f0</code>: <code>i = 0</code> (<code>i</code> lives at <code>[rbp-0x4]</code>), then jump to the
condition at <code>40123f</code>.</li>
<li><code>4011f2</code>–<code>4011ff</code>: <code>rax = in + i</code>, then read <strong>one byte</strong> from there:
<code>eax = in[i]</code>. (<code>in</code> was saved at <code>[rbp-0x18]</code> when the function started.)</li>
<li><code>401202</code>–<code>401209</code>: <code>ecx = i + in[i]</code>.</li>
<li><code>40120b</code>–<code>40121f</code>: <code>eax = i % 4</code> (the <code>and edx,0x3</code> pattern).</li>
<li><code>401221</code>: <code>eax = KEY[i % 4]</code>, one byte from the table at <code>0x402008</code>.</li>
<li><code>401228</code>: <code>ecx = ecx ^ eax</code>: the XOR.</li>
<li><code>40122a</code>–<code>401239</code>: write the low byte of <code>ecx</code> (<code>dl</code>) to <code>out[i]</code>.</li>
<li><code>40123b</code>–<code>401245</code>: <code>i = i + 1</code>; go back to <code>4011f2</code> while <code>i &lt; n</code> (<code>jl</code>,
so exactly <code>n</code> rounds). <code>n</code> was saved at <code>[rbp-0x24]</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Same rule as Ghidra's. Now the data. <code>KEY</code> is at <code>0x402008</code> (4 bytes) and the
stored answer at <code>0x402010</code> (8 bytes, from <code>memcmp</code>):</p>
<pre><code>$ objdump -s -j .rodata toylock-stripped

toylock-stripped:     file format elf64-x86-64

Contents of section .rodata:
 402000 01000200 00000000 1337427f 00000000  .........7B.....
 402010 63553219 76472905 50617373 776f7264  cU2.vG).Password
 402020 3a20000a 00446f6f 72206f70 656e2e00  : ...Door open..
 402030 57726f6e 672e00                      Wrong..
</code></pre>
<p>The row starting <code>402000</code> holds addresses <code>0x402000</code> to <code>0x40200f</code>.
<code>0x402008</code> is 8 bytes into it: skip <code>01000200 00000000</code>, and the next four
bytes are the key, <code>13 37 42 7f</code>. The stored bytes start the next row:
<code>63 55 32 19 76 47 29 05</code>.</p>
<h2>Step 4: dynamic analysis with gdb</h2>
<p>Static analysis gave us a rule. Let us watch it happen, to make sure we read
it right. We stop at the <code>xor</code> (<code>0x401228</code>) and print both operands. Input is
<code>abcdefgh</code> from a file. (Captured with <code>gdb -nx -batch</code>, which turns pwndbg
off so the output is short; each command is shown before its output, and two
<code>[Thread debugging ...]</code> lines gdb prints after <code>run</code> are cut. Your screen
will also show pwndbg's panels.)</p>
<pre><code>$ echo abcdefgh &gt; /tmp/guess.txt
$ gdb -q ./toylock-stripped
(gdb) break *0x401228
Breakpoint 1 at 0x401228
(gdb) run &lt; /tmp/guess.txt
Breakpoint 1, 0x0000000000401228 in ?? ()
(gdb) x/1i $pc
=&gt; 0x401228:    xor    ecx,eax
(gdb) p/x $eax
$1 = 0x13
(gdb) p/x $ecx
$2 = 0x61
(gdb) continue
Breakpoint 1, 0x0000000000401228 in ?? ()
(gdb) p/x $eax
$3 = 0x37
(gdb) continue
Breakpoint 1, 0x0000000000401228 in ?? ()
(gdb) p/x $eax
$4 = 0x42
</code></pre>
<p>Round 0: <code>ecx</code> is <code>0x61</code> (<code>'a'</code> plus 0) and <code>eax</code> is <code>KEY[0] = 0x13</code>. Rounds 1
and 2 bring <code>0x37</code> and <code>0x42</code>: the program is walking the table, as
predicted. (<code>?? ()</code> means gdb has no function name for this address,
because the program is stripped.)</p>
<p>Next, stop at the <code>memcmp</code> call (<code>0x40129d</code>) and look at both buffers. <code>rdi</code>
and <code>rsi</code> hold its first two arguments:</p>
<pre><code>(gdb) delete
(gdb) break *0x40129d
Breakpoint 2 at 0x40129d
(gdb) continue
Breakpoint 2, 0x000000000040129d in ?? ()
(gdb) x/1i $pc
=&gt; 0x40129d:    call   0x4010d0 &lt;memcmp@plt&gt;
(gdb) x/8bx $rdi
0x7fffffffeb38: 0x72    0x54    0x27    0x18    0x7a    0x5c    0x2f    0x10
(gdb) x/8bx $rsi
0x402010:       0x63    0x55    0x32    0x19    0x76    0x47    0x29    0x05
</code></pre>
<p>Check the first byte by hand: <code>('a' + 0) ^ 0x13 = 0x61 ^ 0x13 = 0x72</code>. It
matches the first byte of our scrambled input. The rule is confirmed.</p>
<p><strong>Changing a register.</strong> Right after <code>memcmp</code> returns, <code>check</code> runs
<code>test eax,eax</code> at <code>0x4012a2</code>: <code>eax</code> is 0 only if the buffers matched. In a
fresh gdb session, stop there, overwrite <code>eax</code>, and continue:</p>
<pre><code>(gdb) break *0x4012a2
Breakpoint 1 at 0x4012a2
(gdb) run &lt; /tmp/guess.txt
Breakpoint 1, 0x00000000004012a2 in ?? ()
(gdb) x/1i $pc
=&gt; 0x4012a2:    test   eax,eax
(gdb) p $eax
$1 = 15
(gdb) set $eax = 0
(gdb) p $eax
$2 = 0
(gdb) continue
Password: Door open.
[Inferior 1 (process 30) exited normally]
</code></pre>
<p>With the wrong password, the door opened. This proves we found the decision
point. It did <strong>not</strong> give us the password, and in most challenges it would
not give you the flag either, because a program started by gdb loses its
permission to read <code>/flag</code>.</p>
<h2>Step 5: turn the rule into a script</h2>
<p>The program did <code>out[i] = (in[i] + i) ^ KEY[i % 4]</code> and compared <code>out</code> with
the stored bytes. Undo it in reverse order: XOR first, then subtract <code>i</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python"># Undo toylock's scramble: out[i] = (in[i] + i) ^ KEY[i % 4]
key = bytes.fromhex(&quot;1337427f&quot;)              # the 4-byte table at 0x402008
secret = bytes.fromhex(&quot;6355321976472905&quot;)   # the 8 bytes at 0x402010

password = []
for i, b in enumerate(secret):
    x = b ^ key[i % 4]          # undo the XOR first (it was done last)
    x = (x - i) &amp; 0xff          # then undo &quot;+ i&quot;, keeping one byte
    password.append(x)

print(bytes(password).decode())
</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ python3 undo_toylock.py
pancakes
</code></pre>
<h2>Step 6: confirm on the real program</h2>
<pre><code>$ python3 undo_toylock.py | ./toylock-stripped
Password: Door open.
</code></pre>
<p>Done: the program itself accepted the answer.</p>
<h2>The source, revealed</h2>
<pre><code class="language-c">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

static const unsigned char KEY[4] = { 0x13, 0x37, 0x42, 0x7f };
static const unsigned char SECRET[8] = {
    0x63, 0x55, 0x32, 0x19, 0x76, 0x47, 0x29, 0x05
};

static void scramble(const char *in, unsigned char *out, int n)
{
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)
        out[i] = (in[i] + i) ^ KEY[i % 4];
}

int check(const char *guess)
{
    unsigned char buf[8];

    if (strlen(guess) != 8)
        return 0;
    scramble(guess, buf, 8);
    return memcmp(buf, SECRET, 8) == 0;
}

int main(void)
{
    char line[64];

    printf(&quot;Password: &quot;);
    if (!fgets(line, sizeof line, stdin))
        return 1;
    line[strcspn(line, &quot;\n&quot;)] = '\0';
    if (check(line))
        puts(&quot;Door open.&quot;);
    else
        puts(&quot;Wrong.&quot;);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Built with the same options as this module's challenges, then stripped:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">gcc -O0 -fno-stack-protector -no-pie -fno-pie -o toylock toylock.c
cp toylock toylock-stripped
strip toylock-stripped
</code></pre>
<p>To follow along, save the source as <code>~/toylock.c</code> and run those three
commands in your home directory. If your addresses differ from the ones
above, trust yours.</p>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-6">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-6" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-6" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-6">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">How to read the output</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-6" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-6" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <h2>objdump -d -M intel</h2>
<pre><code>  401221:       0f b6 80 08 20 40 00    movzx  eax,BYTE PTR [rax+0x402008]
  ^^^^^^        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  address       the instruction's       the instruction, Intel syntax:
  (hex)         raw bytes (hex)         DESTINATION first, then SOURCE
</code></pre>
<p>The raw bytes are what the CPU reads. You can usually ignore them, but notice
that the constant <code>0x402008</code> is visible in them backwards (<code>08 20 40 00</code>):
x86-64 is <strong>little-endian</strong> and stores the lowest byte of a number first.</p>
<p>Text after <code>#</code> is objdump's comment, often the address an <code>rip</code>-relative
operand works out to. Text in <code>&lt; &gt;</code> is the nearest known symbol, which in a
stripped program is often wrong.</p>
<h2>Memory operands</h2>
<p>Square brackets mean &quot;the memory at this address&quot;. The word in front says
how many bytes:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>word</th>
<th>bytes</th>
<th>C type, roughly</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>BYTE PTR</code></td>
<td>1</td>
<td><code>char</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WORD PTR</code></td>
<td>2</td>
<td><code>short</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DWORD PTR</code></td>
<td>4</td>
<td><code>int</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>QWORD PTR</code></td>
<td>8</td>
<td><code>long</code>, any pointer</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<ul>
<li><code>DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]</code>: the 4 bytes starting 4 below <code>rbp</code>. At <code>-O0</code> local
variables live at fixed distances below <code>rbp</code>.</li>
<li><code>BYTE PTR [rax+0x402008]</code>: one byte at address <code>0x402008 + rax</code>, a table
read.</li>
<li><code>lea rax,[rbp-0x8]</code> computes the address <code>rbp-0x8</code> and does <strong>not</strong> read
memory. It is how code gets a pointer to a local buffer.</li>
<li><code>movzx</code> reads a small value and fills the rest of the register with zeros.
<code>movsxd</code> copies a signed 4-byte value into an 8-byte register.</li>
</ul>
<p>The stack frame of <code>check</code> in the worked example:</p>
<pre><code> higher addresses
   rbp+0x8    return address (where check goes back to in main)
   rbp        saved rbp of main
   rbp-0x8    buf[0] .. buf[7]       8 bytes; Ghidra calls it local_10
   rbp-0x10   (unused)
   rbp-0x18   copy of the guess pointer (rdi, saved at entry)
   rbp-0x20   &lt;- rsp, after sub rsp,0x20
 lower addresses
</code></pre>
<p>Ghidra's <code>local_10</code> and objdump's <code>rbp-0x8</code> are the same bytes. Ghidra
measures from where the stack pointer was before the function's <code>push rbp</code>,
which is 8 bytes higher than <code>rbp</code>, so its numbers are 8 bigger in functions
like this one.</p>
<h2>Ghidra's names</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>name</th>
<th>meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>FUN_004011d6</code></td>
<td>a function at <code>0x4011d6</code> with no symbol</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DAT_00402010</code></td>
<td>data at <code>0x402010</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>PTR_..._00403fd8</code></td>
<td>a stored address (pointer) at <code>0x403fd8</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>param_1</code>, <code>param_2</code></td>
<td>the function's arguments, in order</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>local_10</code></td>
<td>a local variable on the stack (see above)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>iVar1</code>, <code>uVar3</code>, <code>sVar2</code>, <code>bVar3</code>, <code>pcVar2</code></td>
<td>values Ghidra made up to hold a result: <code>i</code> int, <code>u</code> unsigned or unknown, <code>s</code> <code>size_t</code>, <code>b</code> bool, <code>pc</code> pointer to char</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>undefined8</code>, <code>undefined1</code></td>
<td>&quot;8 bytes (or 1 byte) of something; I don't know the type&quot;</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The decompiler guesses types and can print expressions that are correct but
hard to read. Read carefully around <code>^</code>, <code>&lt;&lt;</code> and casts, and confirm in the
assembly or in gdb when the answer depends on it.</p>
<h2>objdump -s (a section's raw bytes)</h2>
<pre><code> 402000 01000200 00000000 1337427f 00000000  .........7B.....
 ^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
 start  16 bytes, in groups of 4             the same bytes as text
 address                                     ('.' = not printable)
</code></pre>
<p>The bytes are in memory order. To find an array, count bytes from the start
of the row to the array's address: each pair of hex digits is one byte.</p>
<h2>gdb's x command</h2>
<p><code>x/NFU ADDRESS</code> means &quot;examine N units of size U, shown in format F&quot;.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>F</th>
<th>format</th>
<th>U</th>
<th>unit size</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>x</code></td>
<td>hex</td>
<td><code>b</code></td>
<td>1 byte</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>d</code></td>
<td>decimal</td>
<td><code>h</code></td>
<td>2 bytes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>s</code></td>
<td>text string</td>
<td><code>w</code></td>
<td>4 bytes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>i</code></td>
<td>instruction</td>
<td><code>g</code></td>
<td>8 bytes</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The same 8 bytes, the toy's <code>KEY</code> table and the 4 zero bytes after it, three
ways:</p>
<pre><code>(gdb) x/8bx 0x402008
0x402008:       0x13    0x37    0x42    0x7f    0x00    0x00    0x00    0x00
(gdb) x/2wx 0x402008
0x402008:       0x7f423713      0x00000000
(gdb) x/1gx 0x402008
0x402008:       0x000000007f423713
(gdb) x/s 0x402018
0x402018:       &quot;Password: &quot;
</code></pre>
<p><code>x/8bx</code> shows bytes in memory order. <code>x/2wx</code> and <code>x/1gx</code> group them into
numbers, and because of little-endian order the first byte (<code>0x13</code>) becomes
the <strong>last</strong> two hex digits. When you copy bytes out of memory, use the <code>b</code>
form.</p>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-7">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-7" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-7" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-7">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">Glossary</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-7" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-7" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <ul>
<li><strong>address</strong>: a number naming one byte of memory.</li>
<li><strong>argument registers</strong>: <code>rdi</code>, <code>rsi</code>, <code>rdx</code>, <code>rcx</code>, <code>r8</code>, <code>r9</code>, which hold a
function's first six arguments on x86-64 Linux. <code>rax</code>/<code>eax</code> holds the
return value.</li>
<li><strong>attach</strong>: make gdb take control of a process that is already running.</li>
<li><strong>binary</strong>: a compiled program file (as opposed to source code).</li>
<li><strong>breakpoint</strong>: a place where the debugger stops the program.</li>
<li><strong>decode / encode</strong>: turn scrambled bytes back into readable data / the
reverse. Here, a simple reversible rule, not real encryption.</li>
<li><strong>decompiler</strong>: a tool that turns machine code into approximate C (Ghidra).</li>
<li><strong>disassembler</strong>: a tool that turns machine code into assembly text
(<code>objdump</code>).</li>
<li><strong>dynamic analysis</strong>: learning about a program by running it and watching.</li>
<li><strong>ELF</strong>: the file format of Linux programs.</li>
<li><strong>entry point</strong>: the address where a program starts running; shown by
<code>readelf -h</code>.</li>
<li><strong>function pointer</strong>: a stored address of a function, called through a
register (an <strong>indirect call</strong>).</li>
<li><strong>hex (hexadecimal)</strong>: base 16, digits <code>0</code>–<code>9</code> and <code>a</code>–<code>f</code>; <code>0x</code> marks it.
Two hex digits make one byte.</li>
<li><strong>immediate</strong>: a constant written inside an instruction, such as <code>0x3</code> in
<code>and edx,0x3</code>.</li>
<li><strong>index</strong>: the position of an element in an array or table.</li>
<li><strong><code>__libc_start_main</code></strong>: the C library function that <code>_start</code> calls; it
calls <code>main</code>.</li>
<li><strong>little-endian</strong>: storing the lowest byte of a number first in memory.</li>
<li><strong>lookup table</strong>: a stored array of constants that code reads at a computed
position.</li>
<li><strong>loop counter</strong>: the variable (often <code>i</code>) that counts the rounds of a loop.</li>
<li><strong>non-PIE / PIE</strong>: a program that loads at a fixed address / at a random
address each run.</li>
<li><strong>process / PID</strong>: a running copy of a program / the number Linux uses to
name it.</li>
<li><strong>pwndbg</strong>: an add-on that makes gdb show registers, stack and code
automatically.</li>
<li><strong>radare2 (<code>r2</code>)</strong>: a command-line reverse-engineering tool; <code>pdg</code> runs
Ghidra's decompiler inside it.</li>
<li><strong>register</strong>: a small, fast storage slot inside the CPU (<code>rax</code>, <code>rdi</code>, ...).</li>
<li><strong><code>.rodata</code></strong>: the section of a program holding read-only data: constants,
strings, tables.</li>
<li><strong>reverse engineering</strong>: working out how a program works from the program
itself.</li>
<li><strong>setuid</strong>: a file permission that makes a program run with its owner's
rights; here, enough to read <code>/flag</code>.</li>
<li><strong>stack</strong>: memory where a function keeps its local variables; <code>rbp</code> marks
the current function's part of it.</li>
<li><strong><code>_start</code></strong>: the startup code at the entry point.</li>
<li><strong>static analysis</strong>: learning about a program by reading it without running
it.</li>
<li><strong>stripped</strong>: with the symbol table removed.</li>
<li><strong>symbol</strong>: a name attached to an address (<code>main</code>, <code>KEY</code>).</li>
<li><strong><code>.text</code></strong>: the section of a program holding its instructions.</li>
<li><strong>XOR</strong>: exclusive OR; a bit is 1 where the two inputs differ. Applying the
same XOR twice undoes it.</li>
</ul>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
        
  <div class="accordion-item">
    <div class="accordion-item-header" id="module-content-header-8">
      <h2 class="mb-0 button-wrapper">
        <button id="module-content-header-button-8" class="btn btn-link text-decoration-none w-100 challenge-button-2 collapsed " type="button" data-toggle="collapse" data-target="#module-content-body-8" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="module-content-body-8">
          
          
            
            <h4 class="accordion-item-name">
              <i class="resource-icon pr-3 fas fa-file-alt"></i>
              <span class="pr-2">Common mistakes, and where to go next</span>
            </h4>
          
        
        </button>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div id="module-content-body-8" class="collapse" aria-labelledby="module-content-header-8" data-parent="#module-content">
      <div class="accordion-item-body">
        
          
            
            <div class="embed-responsive">
              
              <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Trusting objdump's <code>&lt;name+0x...&gt;</code> labels in a stripped program.</strong> Use the
address. The name is a guess.</li>
<li><strong>Using numbers from someone else's copy.</strong> Each challenge comes in several
versions with different constants. Only the program in your <code>/challenge</code>
counts.</li>
<li><strong>Undoing steps in the wrong order.</strong> If the program added and then XORed,
you XOR first and then subtract.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting <code>&amp; 0xff</code> in Python.</strong> Python numbers never wrap; the program's
bytes do.</li>
<li><strong>Off by one on the loop length.</strong> <code>jl</code> means <code>i &lt; N</code> (N rounds); <code>jle</code>
means <code>i &lt;= N</code> (N + 1 rounds).</li>
<li><strong>Counting <code>.rodata</code> bytes from the start of the row</strong> instead of from the
array's own address.</li>
<li><strong>Reading <code>x/gx</code> or <code>x/wx</code> output as bytes.</strong> Little-endian order reverses
them. Use <code>x/Nbx</code> to copy bytes.</li>
<li><strong><code>break 0x401228</code> without the <code>*</code>.</strong> gdb then looks for a function by that
name. Use <code>break *0x401228</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Expecting the flag while running under gdb.</strong> The debugger removes the
permission to read <code>/flag</code>. Learn in gdb, then run the program normally.
(gdb-runtime is the exception; its README explains why.)</li>
<li><strong>Believing the decompiler over the program.</strong> Ghidra's C is a guess. When
an answer depends on a detail, check the assembly or watch it in gdb.</li>
<li><strong>Giving up on the loop and guessing.</strong> Write down what one round does to
one byte, check it on the first byte by hand (as in the worked example),
then write the script.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where to go next</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://pwn.college/program-security/">pwn.college Program Security dojo</a>:
its <strong>Reverse Engineering</strong> module is the next step up, with many more
crackme-style levels.</li>
<li>Ghidra's own course is already installed: open
<code>/opt/ghidra/docs/GhidraClass/Beginner/</code> in the Desktop workspace, and keep
<code>/opt/ghidra/docs/CheatSheet.html</code> open while you work.</li>
<li><a href="https://beej.us/guide/bggdb/">Beej's Quick Guide to GDB</a>: a short gdb
tutorial.</li>
<li><a href="https://beginners.re/">Reverse Engineering for Beginners</a> by Dennis
Yurichev: a free book that shows what C turns into, compiler by compiler.</li>
<li><a href="https://godbolt.org/">Compiler Explorer</a>: write C and see the assembly
next to it. Choose x86-64 gcc with <code>-O0 -masm=intel</code> to match this module.</li>
<li><a href="https://crackmes.one/">crackmes.one</a>: small practice programs made by
other people, rated by difficulty.</li>
</ul>

            </div>
            
          
        
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

        
      
    
      
        
        
